{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Persistently record sudo-executed commands on node1 in /var/log/sudo-commands.log with user attribution and timestamps.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A validated global sudoers policy directs records to the required file, includes the year, and survives reboot. Direct tests recorded timestamps, invoking user, target user, working directory, and command for both root and a nobody target. The log remained root-owned, mode 0600, correctly labeled, and retained pre-restart data after recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1d2cb8f0a9cf4801","cmd-5f851df0b3be47a0","cmd-3c7b6e0b999a4405","cmd-363f75d874ee428d","cmd-11741c971b284e56"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Keep the dedicated sudo command entries out of the general system log.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before configuration, a sudo marker appeared in the journal. After configuration, unique allowed, pre-reboot, post-reboot, and target-user markers were absent from the journal, /var/log/messages, and /var/log/secure while appearing in the dedicated file.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d737ed5e9bd14caa","cmd-5f851df0b3be47a0","cmd-3c7b6e0b999a4405","cmd-363f75d874ee428d","cmd-11741c971b284e56"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0 were identical before and after execution and reboot. The implementation used the platform's installed sudo and rsyslog packages, with package health remaining successful.","evidence_ids":["cmd-710f01e4fb2a4628","cmd-363f75d874ee428d","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The baseline sudo marker established prior journal behavior; the retained sudoers file and dedicated log directly implement the task; test records were bounded verification; and the reboot was required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. The temporary staging file and transient reboot unit were removed. After reboot there were no failed units, unexpected listeners, damaged package state, or executor staging residue; lifecycle differences were limited to the expected boot identity, process IDs, and systemd private directories.","evidence_ids":["cmd-710f01e4fb2a4628","cmd-d737ed5e9bd14caa","cmd-1d2cb8f0a9cf4801","cmd-5f851df0b3be47a0","cmd-3c7b6e0b999a4405","cmd-34037dd598c84101","cmd-363f75d874ee428d","cmd-11741c971b284e56","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The independent node-restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the restart, became temporarily unavailable, returned with a different boot identity, and recovered its services, policy, preserved log data, platform, and access. Fixed lifecycle observations independently confirm the boot change and healthy final state.","limitations":["The universal claim that every sudo-executed command is recorded is supported by a global Defaults policy and representative root-to-root and root-to-nobody tests, rather than an exhaustive test of every possible identity and sudo policy path.","Log exclusion was directly tested with unique markers against the journal, /var/log/messages, and /var/log/secure; no evidence inventories every conceivable external or custom log sink."]}
